{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role in the history of the Crusader states during the twelfth century. His life was marked by complex political maneuvering and deep involvement in the succession crises that plagued the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He spent many years as a prisoner in Aleppo after being captured by Nur ad-Din and this long captivity shaped his worldview and his approach to diplomacy with Muslim leaders. Upon his release he became one of the most powerful barons in the realm and he often acted as a regent or advisor to kings who were minors or incapacitated by illness.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond inherited his title at a young age following the assassination of his father and he immediately faced threats from neighboring powers. The Nizari Ismailis known as the Assassins targeted his family and this constant danger defined his early rule. His capture in 1164 changed everything because he spent roughly ten years in a Muslim prison where he learned Arabic and developed a nuanced understanding of Islamic culture. This experience made him unique among Latin nobles since he could communicate directly with enemy commanders and negotiate treaties without intermediaries. His time in captivity also gave him a reputation for pragmatism that some contemporaries viewed with suspicion while others saw it as necessary wisdom.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count returned to find a kingdom fractured by internal disputes and he quickly positioned himself as a leader of the noble faction opposed to the court party led by Agnes of Courtenay. He served as bailli or regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not always govern effectively. Raymond sought peace with Saladin and he arranged truces that allowed the kingdom to survive despite growing external pressure. His marriage alliance with Eschiva of Bures gave him control over Galilee and this vast territory increased his influence significantly within the feudal hierarchy. Critics accused him of ambition yet his actions often aimed to preserve the stability of the Christian holdings against overwhelming odds.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy*\nTensions reached a breaking point during the reign of Baldwin V and after the young king died the kingdom descended into chaos that led to the disastrous Battle of Hattin. Raymond opposed the aggressive policies of Guy of Lusignan and he warned against provoking Saladin into open warfare but his counsel went unheeded by the new leadership. He retreated to Tripoli as the Muslim forces swept through the countryside and he died shortly after the catastrophic defeat that lost Jerusalem for the Crusaders. Historians debate whether his withdrawal was an act of despair or a strategic move to save his own lands from total destruction. His legacy remains that of a skilled diplomat who understood the limitations of Crusader power and who tried to navigate an impossible situation with realism rather than religious fervor.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.6927863e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0004346719}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.00032680607}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.01367217}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -3.8028487e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -4.4107533e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "